The implements digital signature verification.  It is designed to
be  simple  and standalone; no external libraries, except for and
are used, in order to ensure maximum  portability.   It  is  com‐
pletely  rewritten  from the version of the program that appeared
in as part of the suite of commands.  The utility requires a file
containing public keys, commonly called a Digitally‐signed infor‐
mation can be fed to in two ways: as standard input, or as  files
provided  on  the  command  line.  The public key part of the key
which was used to sign the file must be present, or the signature
verification will fail.  Files may  be  signed  in  two  distinct
ways:  as  text  documents,  and as binary files.  Text documents
modify the contents to add different line‐ending characters,  and
behave  differently at the final byte of the input document.  Bi‐
nary files are read verbatim, and are not modified  in  any  way.
The  command line argument allows a keyring to be specified.  The
command line argument prints the version of the command and  then
exits.   The  argument allows a to be given, modifying the behav‐
iour of the command.  This command can take one of three  values:
